Z||000002||Nashco||11-25-2001||10:52 PM||||Nope, drilling is it.  There's a special tool made for the fieros to drill out exhaust manifold bolts if they're broken.  Only other way to do it is to remove the head and do it on a bench.  Drilling is it, sorry if that's a bad thing.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>*edited typo*<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 11-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jefrysuko||11-26-2001||02:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Nashco:<BR><B>There's a special tool made for the fieros to drill out exhaust manifold bolts if they're broken.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What tool is this?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jake_Dragon||11-26-2001||06:38 AM||||Do you have a welder? If any of the bolt is sticking out you could weld a nut on it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||11-26-2001||10:27 AM||||My favorite "tool" is called Liquid Wrench.  After several applications of the above tool,  I removed one broken bolt (that stuck out a ways) with a vice-grip pliers.  The other (which broke off flush) I step-drilled and finally it came out with a LH end mill spun CCW. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-26-2001||10:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B> What tool is this?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's just a jig that allows you to accurately drill the bolt (if it's broken flush with the head and you can't get pliers on it).  It was a Pontiac part first, used mostly on recalls.  I can get you a part number if you really want to go and buy one.  There was also a tool manufacturer that made it, but for the life of me I can't remember what their name was right now.  I saw one of the aftermarket tools sell on ebay for over 100 dollars a few weeks ago.  If somebody is really wanting one and can't get it at the dealer, I just may have access to one (PM me if you're interested).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
